---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.5 — De Morgan's Laws &amp; The DungeonGatekeeper Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.2, 2.5, 2.6 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> Compound boolean
    expressions and De Morgan's Laws appear on the AP CS A exam every single year — both in
    Multiple Choice ("Which of the following is equivalent to...") and inside Free Response guard
    conditions. Today you will discover the four traps that catch almost every programmer who
    trusts intuition over truth tables, and build <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code> to pass all
    31 automated checks.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/DungeonGatekeeper.java" download>DungeonGatekeeper.java</a> —
            bare-minimum compiling stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/DungeonGatekeeperCheck.java" download>DungeonGatekeeperCheck.java</a> —
            automated AP-style test suite (31 checks across 5 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac DungeonGatekeeper.java DungeonGatekeeperCheck.java
java DungeonGatekeeperCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>isVaultLockedOut(boolean hasRunekey, boolean isBloodMoon)</code></h3>

    <p>
        The Shadow Vault opens only when a hero holds the Runekey <strong>AND</strong> the Blood
        Moon is rising: <code>hasRunekey &amp;&amp; isBloodMoon</code>. You are writing
        <code>isVaultLockedOut</code>, which returns <code>true</code> when the vault remains
        locked — <strong>without</strong> using an outer <code>!</code> around parentheses.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: Why Distributing <code>!</code> Like Algebra Fails</h3>
        <p>
            In algebra, <code>-(a + b)</code> becomes <code>-a + -b</code>. Many programmers try the
            same thing with <code>!(hasRunekey &amp;&amp; isBloodMoon)</code> and write:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive attempt — put this in isVaultLockedOut and run DungeonGatekeeperCheck:
return !hasRunekey &amp;&amp; !isBloodMoon;</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Run <code>java DungeonGatekeeperCheck</code> with that line. Why did
                <code>(true, true)</code> and <code>(false, false)</code> PASS, while
                <code>(true, false)</code> and <code>(false, true)</code> FAILED?</li>
            <li>State De Morgan's two laws in your own words: what happens to <code>&amp;&amp;</code>
                and <code>||</code> when you push <code>!</code> inside parentheses?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>isVaultLockedOut</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static boolean isVaultLockedOut(boolean hasRunekey, boolean isBloodMoon)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Requirement:</strong> Return the exact De Morgan equivalent of
            <code>!(hasRunekey &amp;&amp; isBloodMoon)</code> with no outer <code>!</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>isInvalidStatScore(int score)</code> &amp; <code>isSafeStatScore(int score)</code></h3>

    <p>
        A legal 5e ability score rolled on 3d6 is between <code>3</code> and <code>18</code>,
        inclusive:
    </p>
    <pre><code>score &gt;= 3 &amp;&amp; score &lt;= 18</code></pre>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 2: The Impossible Range &amp; The Boundary Flip</h3>
        <p>
            When asked to write <code>isInvalidStatScore(int score)</code> (true when
            <code>score</code> is <em>outside</em> <code>3..18</code>), two bugs show up constantly:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Bug A: The Impossible Number
return score &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; score &gt; 18;

// Bug B: The Boundary Thief
return score &lt;= 3 || score &gt;= 18;</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does Bug A return <code>false</code> for <em>every</em> possible integer in Java
                (even <code>-999</code> and <code>999</code>)?</li>
            <li>Why does Bug B fail specifically when <code>score</code> is <code>3</code> or
                <code>18</code>? What is the exact negation of <code>&gt;=</code>, and what is the
                exact negation of <code>&lt;=</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — Stat Range Methods</h4>
    <ul>
        <li><code>public static boolean isSafeStatScore(int score)</code> — returns <code>true</code>
            if <code>score</code> is in the closed interval <code>[3, 18]</code>, <code>false</code>
            otherwise.</li>
        <li><code>public static boolean isInvalidStatScore(int score)</code> — returns <code>true</code>
            if <code>score</code> is outside <code>[3, 18]</code>, written without <code>!</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>doesBossEnrage(boolean lowHp, boolean minionFell, boolean isSilenced)</code></h3>

    <p>
        <strong>The Rule:</strong> The dungeon boss enrages if its HP is low <strong>OR</strong> a
        minion fell this round — <strong>provided the boss is NOT silenced</strong>. If the boss is
        silenced, it can never enrage.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 3: Operator Precedence (<code>&amp;&amp;</code> Binds Tighter Than <code>||</code>)</h3>
        <p>
            Translate the English rule straight into Java without parentheses and run
            <code>DungeonGatekeeperCheck</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Try this in doesBossEnrage:
return lowHp || minionFell &amp;&amp; !isSilenced;</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the 5th check in Part 3:
                <code>doesBossEnrage(true, false, true)</code> — the boss has low HP, no minion fell,
                and the boss <strong>IS silenced</strong>. Your method returned <code>true</code>!
                Why did Java ignore <code>!isSilenced</code> when <code>lowHp</code> was
                <code>true</code>?</li>
            <li>In arithmetic, <code>*</code> has higher precedence than <code>+</code>, so
                <code>a + b * c</code> means <code>a + (b * c)</code>. Between <code>!</code>,
                <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, and <code>||</code>, what is Java's exact order of
                precedence from highest to lowest?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h4>AP Specification — <code>doesBossEnrage</code></h4>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>public static boolean doesBossEnrage(boolean lowHp, boolean minionFell, boolean isSilenced)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Returns <code>true</code> when <code>(lowHp || minionFell)</code>
            is true and <code>isSilenced</code> is false; returns <code>false</code> otherwise.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>hasFairGoldSplit(int partySize, int totalGold, int minShare)</code></h3>

    <p>
        After clearing a room, the party splits <code>totalGold</code> equally among
        <code>partySize</code> heroes. A split is fair if:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>There is at least 1 hero in the party (<code>partySize &gt; 0</code>),</li>
        <li>Each hero's integer share (<code>totalGold / partySize</code>) is at least
            <code>minShare</code>, AND</li>
        <li>The gold divides evenly with <code>0</code> coins left over
            (<code>totalGold % partySize == 0</code>).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — The Short-Circuit Shield</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens in Part 4 of <code>DungeonGatekeeperCheck</code> if you write
                <code>totalGold / partySize &gt;= minShare &amp;&amp; partySize &gt; 0</code> when
                <code>partySize</code> is <code>0</code>? Why does reversing the order of the two
                sides of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> prevent an <code>ArithmeticException</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 5 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 5: <code>classifyEncounterZone(int floor, int partyLevel, boolean hasTorch, String guildRank)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Combine short-circuit null safety, De Morgan logic, and multi-branch conditions to classify a
        dungeon floor into a zone tier:
    </p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Priority</th><th>Condition</th><th>Return Tier</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td>1 (Highest)</td>
            <td><strong>Forbidden / Invalid:</strong> <code>floor &lt;= 0</code>, OR
                <code>partyLevel &lt;= 0</code>, OR <code>guildRank</code> is <code>null</code>, OR
                <code>guildRank</code> equals <code>"Banned"</code></td>
            <td><code>-1</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>2</td>
            <td><strong>Abyssal Boss Zone:</strong> <code>floor &gt;= 10</code> AND
                <code>(partyLevel &gt;= 12 || guildRank.equals("Grandmaster"))</code> AND
                <code>hasTorch</code></td>
            <td><code>3</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>3</td>
            <td><strong>Dark Hazard Zone:</strong> The party does <strong>NOT</strong> have a torch,
                AND it is <strong>NOT</strong> the case that <code>(floor &lt; 5 &amp;&amp; guildRank.equals("Veteran"))</code></td>
            <td><code>2</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>4 (Default)</td>
            <td><strong>Standard Zone:</strong> All other valid cases</td>
            <td><code>1</code></td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Simplifying Priority 3</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the second half of Priority 3:
                <code>!(floor &lt; 5 &amp;&amp; guildRank.equals("Veteran"))</code>. Apply De Morgan's
                Law to rewrite that inner condition without the outer <code>!</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java DungeonGatekeeperCheck</code> reports <strong><code>25 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>All reflection questions above are answered and your <code>.json</code> is downloaded for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/de-morgan.html">De Morgan's Laws</a> — truth tables, range negations, precedence</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Logic</a> — short-circuit evaluation</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="3.5" title="3.5 De Morgan's Laws &amp; The DungeonGatekeeper Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
